    {
      "abstract": ": Large-scale floating offshore wind turbines (FOWTs) in ice-covered seas experiences seakeeping challenges due to their size and compliant mooring systems. To address the computational complexity of predicting these dynamic responses, this study proposes a long short-term memory (LSTM) framework for a 20 MW spar-type FOWT subjected to level ice loads. Training data are generated using SIMA coupled with a validated semi-empirical ice model for three environmental cases corresponding to cut-in, rated and cut-out wind speeds. Two-stage LSTM models are used to study the nonlinear relationship between ice and the FOWT dynamic response. The first-stage LSTM model predicts six degree-of-freedom (DOF) platform motion responses subjected to level ice loads. The second-stage LSTM model predicts the three mooring lines tensions of the FOWT based on the platform motion responses with displacement-only, displacement-velocity and displacement-acceleration inputs compared. A novel weighted roll-out strategy is used to achieve stable long-term predictions beyond the trained horizon. The LSTM framework reproduces dominant low-frequency characteristics of the platform motions with correlation coefficients of 0.79∼0.97, 0.6∼0.96 and 0.75∼0.92 for the three cases, respectively, while amplitude and phase errors remain in longer roll-outs. The mooring line tension predictions show good agreement with the simulation results. Using the LSTM predicted motions leads to higher prediction errors in the mooring line tension than using the SIMA simulated motions, demonstrating the influence of upstream motion-prediction errors on the second-stage tension estimates. These results support the feasibility of a data-driven surrogate for rapid response prediction within the investigated configuration and environmental conditions.",
